  "mechanisms": [
    "Light has important effects on human physiology unrelated to visual perception, influencing non-visual biological effects through melanopsin-containing intrinsically photoreceptive retinal ganglion cells (ipRGC). These effects have physiological and cognitive impacts on arousal, alertness, emotional responses, mood regulation, and learning. The study suggests that task performance and neurophysiological metrics (EEG, HRV, EDA) go in the same direction, indicating that lower illuminance (100 lx) leads to higher neurophysiological activation and better memory performance."
  ],
  "moderators": [
    "Time of day (not controlled, potential moderator)",
    "Other lighting variables (e.g., uniformity, colour temperature) (not examined, potential moderators)",
    "VR-induced focus on enjoyment vs task performance (potential moderator)",
    "Presence of other students and viewpoint positions within the classroom (not examined, potential moderators)"
  ],
  "implementation_implications": [
    "The findings have important consequences for the energy efficiency of educational centres, suggesting that lower illuminance levels (e.g., 100 lx) may improve learning outcomes while reducing energy consumption.",
    "The results can inform architects and designers in establishing lighting design guidelines that enhance students' cognitive processes in learning environments.",
    "The study provides information about the cognitive implications of lighting for learning in virtual environments."
  ],
  "domains": [
    "A4_Light",
    "A9_Task_Cognition",
    "A10_Temporal"
  ],
